WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.898
Welcome to lesson three: keeping agents alive when things break.

2
00:00:04.228 --> 00:00:08.765
An agent relies on a model, tools, and data stores, and any of them can fail.

3
00:00:09.565 --> 00:00:12.170
Picture a travel agent rebooking passengers

4
00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:14.763
after a storm cancels hundreds of flights.

5
00:00:15.093 --> 00:00:18.392
Then the airline's seat-availability service slows to a crawl.

6
00:00:19.192 --> 00:00:23.003
Every rebooking hangs on that one service, until no workers are left.

7
00:00:23.333 --> 00:00:26.782
Now even passengers who only wanted a hotel get nothing.

8
00:00:27.582 --> 00:00:30.405
The first pattern is a timeout on every external call,

9
00:00:30.455 --> 00:00:32.161
shorter than the caller's budget.

10
00:00:32.491 --> 00:00:35.620
A tool with no timeout can hang an agent run forever.

11
00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:39.401
The second is bounded retries: backoff with jitter,

12
00:00:39.451 --> 00:00:42.194
capped attempts, and only for transient errors.

13
00:00:42.524 --> 00:00:46.719
Never retry a four-hundred-class error, because wrong arguments stay wrong.

14
00:00:47.519 --> 00:00:50.836
The third is a circuit breaker: after repeated failures,

15
00:00:50.886 --> 00:00:53.421
stop calling and fail fast for a cool-down.

16
00:00:53.751 --> 00:00:56.539
That way, one slow service can't swallow every worker.

17
00:00:57.339 --> 00:01:01.449
The fourth is bulkheads: a separate connection pool for each dependency.

18
00:01:01.779 --> 00:01:05.846
If the seat service fills its pool, hotel and baggage requests still flow.

19
00:01:06.646 --> 00:01:11.972
Beware the retry storm, where the agent retries while its HTTP client retries too.

20
00:01:12.302 --> 00:01:13.877
Let one layer own retries,

21
00:01:13.927 --> 00:01:17.628
make side-effecting tools idempotent, and always add jitter.

22
00:01:18.428 --> 00:01:20.041
When the seat service is down,

23
00:01:20.091 --> 00:01:23.477
degrade gracefully instead of erroring or making something up.

24
00:01:23.807 --> 00:01:27.575
Serve cached seats with their age shown, or fall back to a smaller model.

25
00:01:28.375 --> 00:01:32.912
Failing that, answer a narrower question, queue the request, or hand off to a human.

26
00:01:33.242 --> 00:01:37.053
The one unacceptable behavior is answering as if nothing were wrong.

27
00:01:37.853 --> 00:01:42.497
Keep session state in Redis or Postgres, so any replica can serve any request.

28
00:01:42.827 --> 00:01:45.955
That beats sticky sessions, which break when pods scale down.

29
00:01:46.755 --> 00:01:49.027
Checkpoint after each significant step,

30
00:01:49.077 --> 00:01:51.825
so a crashed run resumes instead of restarting.

31
00:01:52.155 --> 00:01:56.671
And give every run a durable ID, so a complaint traces to its exact path.

32
00:01:57.471 --> 00:01:58.762
For high availability,

33
00:01:58.812 --> 00:02:02.541
run inference in two regions and keep a backup model provider.

34
00:02:02.871 --> 00:02:05.839
Replicate the vector index, and test failover,

35
00:02:05.889 --> 00:02:08.922
because untested failover is just a hypothesis.

36
00:02:09.722 --> 00:02:12.463
On the exam, the complete answer names a timeout,

37
00:02:12.513 --> 00:02:16.051
bounded retries with jitter, a circuit breaker, and a fallback.

38
00:02:16.381 --> 00:02:19.616
Retries alone are the trap, so read on for the details.
